      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\), we need to analyze the behavior of the function by considering different cases based on the critical points where the expressions inside the absolute values change sign. These critical points are \\( x = -1 \\) and \\( x = 1 \\).\n\nWe will break the real number line into three intervals: \\( (-\\infty, -1) \\), \\( (-1, 1) \\), and \\( (1, \\infty) \\).\n\n**Case 1: \\( x < -1 \\)**\n\nFor \\( x < -1 \\), both \\( x+1 \\) and \\( x-1 \\) are negative. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ |x+1| = -(x+1) = -x-1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1 \\]\nSo, the function becomes:\n\\[ G(x) = (-x-1) - (-x+1) = -x-1 + x-1 = -2 \\]\nThus, for \\( x < -1 \\), \\( G(x) = -2 \\).\n\n**Case 2: \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\)**\n\nFor \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), \\( x+1 \\) is non-negative and \\( x-1 \\) is negative. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ |x+1| = x+1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1 \\]\nSo, the function becomes:\n\\[ G(x) = (x+1) - (-x+1) = x+1 + x-1 = 2x \\]\nThus, for \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), \\( G(x) = 2x \\). As \\( x \\) varies from \\(-1\\) to \\(1\\), \\( G(x) \\) varies from \\(2(-1) = -2\\) to \\(2(1) = 2\\).\n\n**Case 3: \\( x \\geq 1 \\)**\n\nFor \\( x \\geq 1 \\), both \\( x+1 \\) and \\( x-1 \\) are non-negative. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ |x+1| = x+1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = x-1 \\]\nSo, the function becomes:\n\\[ G(x) = (x+1) - (x-1) = x+1 - x+1 = 2 \\]\nThus, for \\( x \\geq 1 \\), \\( G(x) = 2 \\).\n\nCombining the results from all three cases, we see that the function \\( G(x) \\) takes on all values in the interval \\([-2, 2]\\). Therefore, the range of the function is:\n\\[ \\boxed{[-2, 2]} \\]",
